course: religion-ethics
lesson: 4
title: Abraham, Isaac and Jacob
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: God called Abram (later Abraham) to leave his home for a land God would show him, promising to make him a great nation with descendants as many as the stars; Jews, Christians and Muslims all honour him as a father of faith.
explain: Sarah laughed when told she would have a son in old age, but Isaac, whose name means "he laughs", was born; Abraham also welcomed three strangers with food and rest, who turned out to be God's messengers.
explain: In a hard story, God told Abraham to offer Isaac, then stopped him and provided a ram instead; many read it as showing that God does not want child sacrifice and that God provides.
explain: Isaac's son Jacob tricked his twin Esau out of his birthright and blessing, ran away, dreamed of a ladder to heaven, later wrestled all night and was renamed Israel; his twelve sons became the twelve tribes.
explain: Years later Esau ran to meet Jacob and hugged him instead of taking revenge.
explain: For a child today: faith means trusting without knowing every step, welcome strangers kindly, cheating breaks trust, and forgiveness can heal even old family hurts.
fact: God told Abram to leave his home for a land God would show him, promising to make him a great nation.
fact: Jews, Christians and Muslims all honour Abraham as a father of their faith; Muslims call him Ibrahim.
fact: God told Abraham to look at the stars and try to count them: his descendants would be as many.
fact: Sarah laughed when told she would have a son in old age; their son was named Isaac, meaning "he laughs".
fact: Abraham welcomed three travellers with food and rest; they turned out to be messengers of God.
fact: God told Abraham to offer his son Isaac, then stopped him and provided a ram instead.
fact: Many read the testing of Abraham as showing that God does not want child sacrifice and that he provides; it is a hard story people still discuss.
fact: Jacob and Esau were twin brothers, the sons of Isaac and Rebekah.
fact: Jacob tricked Esau out of his birthright and his father's blessing.
fact: Running away from Esau, Jacob dreamed of a ladder to heaven with angels on it, and God promised to be with him.
fact: Jacob wrestled all night with a mysterious man and was renamed Israel, a name often explained as "he struggles with God".
fact: Jacob's twelve sons became the twelve tribes of Israel.
fact: Years later Esau ran to meet Jacob and hugged him instead of taking revenge.
exercise: What did God ask Abraham to do when he called him? | Leave his home for a land God would show him | Build a great wooden boat for all the animals | Build a tall tower of bricks to reach heaven | Go down to Egypt and become a great ruler there
exercise: What does Abraham's call teach about faith? | Faith means trusting God enough to set out without knowing every step | Faith means you must know the whole plan first | Faith means staying where you are always | Faith means never asking God anything
exercise: Which faiths honour Abraham as a father of their faith? | Judaism, Christianity and Islam | Only Christianity | Hinduism and Buddhism, but not the others | Only Judaism
exercise: Why is sharing Abraham a reason for respect between faiths? | The three faiths share a common root | It proves the three faiths are exactly the same | It means only one of them is right about him | Abraham told them never to talk to each other
exercise: What does the promise of the stars teach? | God's promises can be bigger than what seems possible | God's promises are always small | Abraham was a famous astronomer who counted every star | Stars decide what happens to people
exercise: Why did Sarah laugh? | She was told she would have a son in old age | Abraham had just told her a really funny joke | She saw the three strangers trip and fall over | She was so happy to be leaving her old home behind
exercise: What does Sarah's story teach? | Nothing is too hard for God; doubt can turn into joy | Laughing at God always brings punishment | People should never have children when they are old, the story says | Doubt always stays doubt forever
exercise: What did Abraham do when three strangers came by? | He welcomed them with food and rest | He sent them away because they were strangers | He asked them to pay for water | He hid from them in his tent
exercise: What does the story of Abraham and the strangers teach? | Hospitality to strangers is a holy thing | Strangers are always dangerous | Only friends deserve food and rest | Messengers of God always look like angels
exercise: How did the testing of Abraham end? | God stopped him and provided a ram instead | Abraham refused to listen to God at all and stayed at home | Isaac ran away into the desert | Abraham sold Isaac to some traders
exercise: How do many people read the testing of Abraham? | God does not want child sacrifice, and God provides; it is still a hard story | God wants parents to hurt their children | It is a funny story about a ram | It shows that Abraham did not love Isaac
exercise: What did Jacob trick Esau out of? | His birthright and his father's blessing | His fine striped coat and his flocks of sheep | His tent in the hills and his herds of camels | His hunting bow and all of his best arrows
exercise: What does the story of Jacob and Esau teach about cheating? | It may win something for a while but breaks trust and families | Cheating is clever and always worth it | Cheating is fine between brothers | Cheating only matters if you happen to be caught by someone in charge
exercise: What did Jacob see in his dream when running away? | A ladder to heaven with angels on it | Seven fat cows and seven thin cows | A burning bush that did not burn up | A rainbow over the sea
exercise: What does Jacob's ladder teach? | God is with people even when they have made mistakes | God only visits people who never do anything wrong at all | Ladders are holy objects | Dreams always tell the future exactly
exercise: What happened when Jacob wrestled all night? | He was renamed Israel | He lost and had to go back home | He became king of Egypt | He forgot about his brother
exercise: What did Esau do when he met Jacob years later? | He ran to him and hugged him instead of taking revenge | He took back his birthright by force | He refused to look at him | He sent all his men to chase Jacob away and never come back
exercise: What does the meeting of Jacob and Esau teach? | Forgiveness can heal even old and deep family hurts | Old hurts can never be healed | It is better to never meet your family again | You should forgive someone only if you get something back for it
test: my cousin reckons Abraham was only a Jewish thing, nothing to do with Muslims. right? | No, Jews, Christians and Muslims all honour Abraham as a father of faith | Yes, only Jews know about Abraham | No, only Muslims know about Abraham | Yes, and Christians have never heard of him
test: Yusuf, who is Muslim, says, "We call him Ibrahim." What does this show? | The three faiths share Abraham as a common root, a reason for respect | Ibrahim must be a different man | Muslims copied the story of Abraham out of a film and changed his name | Christians should not talk about Ibrahim
test: Your family moves to a new country and you don't know anyone. Mum says, "We'll trust God and take it one step at a time." Who is she like? | Abraham, who set out without knowing every step | Esau, who took his revenge on his twin brother Jacob | The builders of Babel, who wanted fame and glory | Jacob, who tricked his old father to get a blessing
test: New kid sits alone at lunch, my mate goes "ignore him, we don't know him." what would Abraham do | Welcome him, as Abraham welcomed the three strangers | Ignore him, strangers are trouble | Ask him to pay for a seat first | Wait till a teacher says he's alright, then maybe talk to him
test: My brother copied my homework and got a gold star, and he goes "ha, I won". What does Jacob's story say? | Cheating may win for a while but breaks trust | Cheating is fine if it gets you a prize | He was right, winning the gold star is all that matters | Cheating only matters if a teacher sees
test: Eli, who has no religion, says: "The story where God tells Abraham to kill his son is horrible." What is a respectful answer? | It is a hard story; many read it as showing God does not want child sacrifice, since he stopped Abraham | You're not allowed to say that about the Bible | Yes, so Christians think hurting children is fine | It's a funny story, you're taking it too seriously
test: Your little sister says: "Grandad was so mean to Dad years ago, they'll never make up." What story gives hope? | Jacob and Esau made peace after many years | Cain and Abel, who never got to make peace at all | The Tower of Babel, where people were scattered | Noah's flood, which covered the whole earth
test: "I messed up so bad, God won't wanna know me now," says your friend after lying to his mum. Which story helps? | Jacob's ladder: God was still with Jacob after his mistakes | Babel: God scatters the proud | The flood: everyone left outside the ark was lost in the waters | Sarah: she laughed at God's promise
test: Mum says "My doctor says I can't have more children, but we'll see." A gran says, "Sarah's story says nothing is too hard for God." What did Sarah do first? | She laughed in doubt, then her son Isaac was born | She refused to believe it and left Abraham to live on her own | She asked the strangers for medicine | She counted the stars every night
test: Olympiad judge: "Why might Jacob have been given the name Israel after wrestling all night?" | Struggling honestly with God is part of faith | He won a fighting competition | His old name was too hard to say | He wanted to hide from Esau with a new name
test: Judge's follow-up: "Esau had every reason for revenge. Why is his hug the stronger choice?" | Revenge would have kept the family broken; forgiveness healed it | Because Esau had forgotten what happened | Because Jacob had paid him a lot of money | Because forgiveness means just pretending nothing had ever gone wrong
exam: Identify the principal virtue exemplified by Abraham in departing from his homeland at the divine command. | Trust in God without knowledge of every particular | Ambition to establish himself as a ruler over other nations | Desire to escape a famine which had afflicted his homeland | Obedience to the express instructions of his earthly father
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the regard in which Abraham is held. | He is honoured as a father of faith by Jews, Christians and Muslims | He is honoured exclusively within the Christian tradition | He is honoured exclusively within the Islamic tradition | He is regarded by all three faiths as a figure of no significance whatever
exam: Ascertain the means by which, according to Genesis, Jacob obtained the birthright and blessing belonging to Esau. | By deception practised upon his brother and his father | By victory over Esau in single combat | By lawful purchase from the father, Isaac | By the voluntary and cheerful gift of Esau
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of Esau upon meeting Jacob after many years? | He embraced his brother rather than exacting vengeance | He reclaimed his birthright by force of arms | He declined to speak with his brother at all | He demanded payment in compensation for his loss
